--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party components
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Labtris depends on the following, none of which impose obligations beyond
attribution on a distribution of this software.

pyroute2 is dual-licensed GPL-2.0-or-later OR Apache-2.0. Labtris takes it
under Apache-2.0. This matters: labtris_netd imports pyroute2 directly, so
the GPL arm would make that daemon's own licence GPL. If you fork Labtris and
relicense, check this first.

certifi is MPL-2.0. That is file-level copyleft — distributing it alongside
Apache-2.0 code is fine; modifying certifi's own files obliges you to publish
those changes.

Everything else is BSD, MIT, Apache-2.0 or PSF: fastapi, starlette, pydantic,
uvicorn, sqlalchemy, alembic, asyncpg, aiodocker, structlog, ulid-py,
websockets, pyyaml, cryptography, PyJWT and their transitive dependencies.

Labtris invokes, but does not link against or redistribute:

  QEMU            GPL-2.0        run as a subprocess
  Wireshark       GPL-2.0        run as a subprocess
  tcpdump         BSD-3-Clause   run as a subprocess
  guacd           Apache-2.0     spoken to over a socket

Running a GPL program as a separate process does not make the caller a
derivative work. Bundling those binaries into a distribution image is a
different question, and the ISO work will need to answer it.

The browser bundle includes guacamole-common-js (Apache-2.0).
